1. Introduction and Claims Ledger

1.1 The problem, restated

The expansion of artificial intelligence infrastructure exhibits a measurable gap between manufactured computational hardware and energizable computational capacity. The theoretical object of this monograph is not the average utilisation of installed accelerators, but the joint distribution of energisation delay and capital loss. The central claim is financial: a deployment pipeline with correlated stages converts lead-time mismatch into a balance-sheet exposure that is measurable, reportable, and policy-relevant before it is physically visible.

This is a theoretical monograph. It reports no experiments, no physical tests, and no code training. Every numerical anchor is drawn from public records or published research and is tagged in the claims ledger below. The purpose of the ledger is to separate what the framework requires from what the evidence currently supports, so that the analytical structure survives even if individual anchors are revised.

