Three corrections follow directly from the ledger and are incorporated throughout.

First, the LBNL 61-month and 22-month figures are pooled generator medians for built projects, not PJM load-interconnection medians and not data-centre-specific. They establish a secular increase in time-to-build for generation, which is the relevant upstream constraint for energisation, but they cannot be used as a PJM load statistic. The PJM "more than eight years" figure is secondary and should carry an interval.

Second, the Nscale loss is not a stranded-capital measurement. The $1.02 bn figure includes $457.1 mn of fair-value remeasurement on warrants held by Nvidia and Sandton, an accounting entry with no immediate cash outflow. The TCV is customers' take-or-pay revenue, not Nscale's exposure. The 461,000 "active or contracted" GPUs are overwhelmingly facilities under development, not delivered hardware sitting idle. Nscale is therefore an illustration of the construction-pipeline structure the model predicts—contracted obligations against capacity not yet energised—but it is not a validation case for Σ. It is removed from that role.

Third, the depreciation evidence does not support a single 24–27% annual rate. It supports a vintage-dependent residual-value curve: older architectures decay at roughly 20–25% per year, current architectures at roughly 12–15% per year, and the newest architectures can appreciate in the secondary market because supply is constrained relative to demand. A framework that assumes uniform fast depreciation will overstate stranded capital for current-generation hardware and understate it for legacy inventory.

1.3 What is retained, revised, and discarded

Retained: the lead-time differential as the central object; the separation of manufactured, installed, and operational capacity; the separation of fast and slow state; tiered critical services; the multiplanetary feasibility filter.

Revised: the pipeline is a stochastic activity network with correlated durations, not a max of independent stages; abandonment is endogenous to the interaction between inventory and the cost of capital, but the mechanism runs through the energisation service rate; stranded capital is measured as foregone contribution margin plus contractual exposure, not as flat accounting depreciation; common-mode failure is modelled as topological propagation through a shared electrical corridor, with a copula only for the residual financial correlation; survivability is computed by a control-aware dynamic program with a stated lower bound.

Discarded: independent lead-time distributions; the claim that §2.2 of v0.3 produces bistability; the DP recursion of v0.3 that returns zero on every safe state; the double-counting of depreciation in Σ; the use of Nscale as a validation case for Σ; the single 24–27% depreciation rate; the GAO 1,200–6,000 m²/MW anchor as corroboration.

