2. The Coupled Pipeline as a Stochastic Activity Network

2.1 Activity network representation

Let a project consist of activities indexed by i \in \mathcal{A}, with durations D_i > 0. The project completion time is the length of the longest path through the activity network:

T_{\mathrm{op}} = t_0 + \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}} \sum_{i \in p} D_i,

where \mathcal{P} is the set of directed paths from project start to energisation. The activities include hardware delivery H, facility construction B, grid interconnection study and agreement G_s, network upgrade construction G_u, and commissioning C. The precedence constraints are not a simple chain: construction can proceed while interconnection study is pending, but energisation cannot occur until both the facility and the grid connection are complete. The path structure therefore encodes re-sequencing, which the max-of-independent-stages formulation could not represent.

2.2 Correlated durations

The duration vector is

\mathbf{D} = (D_H, D_B, D_{G_s}, D_{G_u}, D_C) \sim F_{\boldsymbol{\theta}},

where F_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} is a joint distribution with dependence induced by three channels: shared equipment supply chains (transformers, turbines, switchgear), shared financing conditions (the cost of capital affects the pace of all activities), and shared labour and permitting markets. The dependence is not a modelling convenience; a generator step-up transformer delay blocks commissioning even when the building shell is complete, and a financing delay extends all activities simultaneously because none proceeds without capital commitment.

For calibration, the marginal distributions are specified as follows. D_{G_s} is fitted to LBNL interconnection-queue duration data, which shows a secular increase from 22 months in 2008 to 61 months in 2025 for built generators, with a heavy right tail for projects that enter multiple study cycles. D_{G_u} is fitted to transformer and turbine lead-time data, with the heavy-frame gas turbine distribution carrying a substantially heavier tail than the transformer distribution. D_H is comparatively tight because accelerator shipments are measured in months, but it is not degenerate because allocation of constrained supply is itself a queue.

The dependence structure is represented by a vine copula on the activity durations, with pair-copula families selected by tail behaviour. The key dependence parameters are \rho_{HG} (hardware and grid-upgrade durations), \rho_{BG} (build and grid-upgrade durations), and \rho_{HF} (hardware and financing conditions). The first two are positive because shared supply chains and shared financing propagate delays; the third is positive because tighter financing lengthens all procurement and construction activities.

2.3 The lead-time differential

Define the energisation lag for a project as

\Delta_L = T_{\mathrm{op}} - t_{\mathrm{hardware\ available}},

the difference between the date at which the hardware could physically be installed and the date at which it can be energised. The v0.2 formulation treated \Delta_L as a deterministic differential between two lead times. The activity-network formulation makes \Delta_L a random variable whose distribution is inherited from the joint duration distribution. The framework's empirical claim is not that \mathbb{E}[\Delta_L] > 0 in all regions at all times, but that in specific regions and windows the distribution of \Delta_L is stochastically positive and its right tail is thick enough to interact with the depreciation clock.

