3. Endogenous Abandonment and the Bistability Condition

3.1 Why the v0.3 abandonment equation has no threshold

Version 0.3 wrote inventory dynamics as

\dot{I}(t) = u_H(t) - a(t) - \left[\theta_0 + \theta_1 I(t) r(t)\right] I(t).

For fixed service rate a and fixed cost of capital r, the right-hand side is concave and decreasing in I: the derivative with respect to I is -\theta_0 - 2\theta_1 r I < 0. The equation therefore has a unique stable equilibrium for given u_H and a, not two. The quadratic term produces stronger abandonment at higher inventory, which is negative feedback, not self-reinforcement. The claim in v0.3 that the pipeline exhibits a threshold between low-inventory and high-inventory equilibria does not follow from this equation.

3.2 The corrected mechanism

Self-reinforcement requires the energisation service rate to fall as inventory and financing strain rise. The economically natural channel is that a large unenergised inventory raises the operator's cost of capital and absorbs balance-sheet capacity, which in turn slows the grid-interconnection and construction activities that would energise the inventory. Write

a(t) = a_0 \, Q(t) \, \Psi\!\left(r(I(t))\right),

where Q(t) is the number of projects at the head of the queue with completed construction, a_0 is a baseline service intensity, and \Psi is a decreasing function of the project-weighted cost of capital:

\Psi'(r) < 0, \qquad r'(I) > 0.

The cost of capital is increasing in inventory because a larger unenergised stock raises leverage, worsens covenant headroom, and increases the spread demanded by lenders. Substituting into the inventory equation:

\dot{I}(t) = u_H(t) - a_0 Q(t) \Psi\!\left(r(I(t))\right) - \left[\theta_0 + \theta_1 I(t) r(I(t))\right] I(t).

The right-hand side is now non-monotone in I: at low inventory, the service rate is high and abandonment is low; at high inventory, the service rate falls and abandonment rises, so the net inflow can remain positive even as inventory grows. There can be two stable equilibria separated by an unstable threshold. This is the formal counterpart of the observation that stranded capital can become self-reinforcing once financing costs rise against a large unenergised stock.

Proposition 1 (Bistability under financing-constrained energisation). Suppose \Psi is strictly decreasing and convex, r is strictly increasing and convex, and \theta_1 > 0. Then for an open set of parameters (u_H, a_0, \theta_0, \theta_1), the inventory dynamics admit two stable equilibria I_L^* < I_H^* separated by an unstable equilibrium I_U^*. The low-inventory equilibrium is characterised by high service rate and low abandonment; the high-inventory equilibrium by low service rate and high abandonment.

The proof follows from the shape of the right-hand side: the inflow u_H is constant, while the outflow is the sum of a decreasing service term and an increasing abandonment term. For intermediate values of u_H, the outflow curve is S-shaped in I, crossing the inflow line three times. The threshold I_U^* is the tipping point.

3.3 Interpretation

The threshold is not a property of inventory alone. It is a property of the interaction between inventory and financing conditions. The same inventory level can be stable in a low-rate environment and unstable in a high-rate environment. This is why the Nscale structure—large contracted obligations against a small active revenue base—is consistent with the model without being a measurement of \Sigma: it is a firm positioned near the threshold, with the cost of capital determining which side of the threshold it occupies.

