4. Stranded Capital Without Double Counting

4.1 The double-counting error

Version 0.3 defined

\Sigma(T) = \int_0^T \left[\kappa_h I(t) v_h + \kappa_c I_c(t) v_c\right] dt.

This double counts. Idle hardware depreciates physically and technologically whether or not it is energised. The accounting depreciation of an unenergised GPU is not a social cost created by the energisation delay; it is a cost that would have been incurred anyway. The stranded component is the difference between the value the asset would have created if energised at the earliest feasible date and the value it actually creates at the date it is energised. That difference is a foregone contribution margin, not a depreciation charge.

4.2 Corrected definition

Let t_i^H be the date at which unit i of hardware becomes available, and let t_i^E be its energisation date. Let v_h be the purchase value per unit and let \pi(t) be the contribution margin per unit of energised capacity per unit time (revenue minus variable operating cost, including energy). The stranded capital associated with the delay is

\Sigma(T) = \sum_i \int_{t_i^H}^{\min(t_i^E,T)} e^{-r t} \left[\pi(t) - c_{\mathrm{idle}}\right] dt \;+\; \int_0^T e^{-r t} \kappa_c I_c(t) v_c \, dt,

where c_{\mathrm{idle}} is the carrying cost of idle hardware (warehousing, insurance, financing) and the second term is contractual take-or-pay exposure. The first term is step-like: it is zero before t_i^H, positive during the delay interval, and zero again after energisation. It is not a flat depreciation rate applied to inventory.

This definition has three advantages. First, it does not count depreciation that would have occurred anyway. Second, it makes the dependence on the contribution margin explicit: hardware that would have been economically obsolete at energisation contributes little, so the stranded cost is small; hardware that would have earned a high margin contributes a lot. Third, it separates the physical clock from the financial clock: the depreciation schedule affects the residual value of the asset, but the stranded cost is the foregone flow, not the asset's decline in book value.

4.3 Vintage-dependent residual values

The empirical evidence does not support a single depreciation rate. The secondary-market retention curves imply that the economic depreciation rate is vintage-specific:

\delta(v) = -\frac{1}{R_v(a)} \frac{dR_v}{da},

where R_v(a) is the residual value of vintage v at age a. For older architectures, \delta is roughly 0.20–0.25 per year. For current architectures, \delta is roughly 0.12–0.15 per year. For the newest architectures, \delta can be negative in the first year because supply constraints raise secondary-market prices above purchase prices. A framework that applies a single \kappa_h to all inventory will misstate \Sigma for both tails.

The correct treatment is to carry a vintage distribution of inventory and apply \delta(v) to each vintage. The aggregate stranded cost is the sum over vintages of the delay interval times the vintage-specific contribution margin. This is more demanding computationally but it is the only specification consistent with the observed residual-value curves.

4.4 Contractual exposure

The take-or-pay term \kappa_c I_c(t) v_c is separate and can dominate. A hyperscaler that has committed to a power purchase agreement or a colocation contract for capacity it cannot energise owes the contract regardless of whether the GPUs are running. \kappa_c is a contractual rate, not a physical one, and it enters \Sigma even when I(t) is zero because the hardware has been abandoned. The public data on take-or-pay terms are incomplete. The framework therefore treats \kappa_c as a stress parameter rather than an estimated parameter, and reports \Sigma as a function of the ratio \kappa_c/\kappa_h rather than at a single point.

