5. Financial Survivability and the Corrected Dynamic Program

5.1 The v0.3 recursion error

Version 0.3 wrote the survivability recursion as

V_t(x) = \min\left\{\mathbf{1}\{x \notin \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{crit}}\},\; \mathbb{E}\left[V_{t+1}(x') \mid x\right]\right\}.

This is not a survivability recursion. For any safe state x \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{crit}}, the indicator is zero, so the minimum is zero regardless of the continuation value. The recursion returns zero everywhere and carries no control. It cannot be used to compute a survival probability.

5.2 The corrected recursion

Survivability is the probability of remaining in the safe set over the horizon, optimised over admissible controls. The correct backward recursion is

V_t(x) = \mathbf{1}\{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{crit}}\} \cdot \max_{u \in \mathcal{U}(x)} \mathbb{E}\left[V_{t+1}(x') \mid x, u\right],

with terminal condition V_T(x) = \mathbf{1}\{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{crit}}\}. If the state is outside the safe set, the value is zero because survival has already failed. If the state is inside, the controller chooses the action that maximises the expected continuation value. The survivability of the system from initial state x_0 is \mathcal{V}(x_0,T) = V_0(x_0).

The control set \mathcal{U}(x) includes curtailment of deferrable load, discharging storage, ramping behind-the-meter generation, and shifting workload between facilities. The Tier 1 floor is encoded in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{crit}}: the safe set is the set of states in which Tier 1 services can be served at the required reliability.

5.3 The semi-analytic lower bound

For linear fast dynamics with a polygonal safe set, the survivability admits a semi-analytic lower bound derived from the viability kernel of the deterministic system and a bound on the disturbance. The bound is computed by solving a linear program at each grid point, which is substantially cheaper than a full Monte Carlo. The deliverable is the gap between this lower bound and a Monte Carlo estimate at representative initial states, reported as a function of the grid resolution and the number of Monte Carlo paths.

The bound is not a substitute for the exact computation; it is a certificate. If the lower bound exceeds the governance threshold 1-\alpha for Tier 1, the system is certified survivable without a full simulation. If the lower bound falls below the threshold while the Monte Carlo estimate exceeds it, the gap itself is the object of study, because it indicates that the bound is too conservative for the operating regime.

