6. Common-Mode Failure: From Copula to Topology-Conditioned Dependence

6.1 Why the t-copula is the wrong primary model

The Ashburn event is not a correlation problem in the copula sense. A single transmission fault on a 230 kV line caused approximately 3.8 GW of data-centre load to disconnect from PJM because the facilities shared a common electrical topology: the same transmission corridor, the same protection zone, or the same voltage-sensitive load behaviour. The facilities did not fail independently and then happen to fail together; they failed through a shared network. A t-copula can fit the joint failure indicators, but it does not represent the propagation mechanism, and therefore it will mispredict the effect of network reconfiguration, protection-coordination changes, or geographic diversification.

The NERC record supports this reading. The Level 3 alert is about computational load behaviour during faults—voltage-sensitive load reductions, protection interactions, and post-fault recovery—not about pairwise correlation between facilities. The relevant dependence is topological, not statistical.

6.2 A two-layer model

The common-mode failure model should have two layers. The topological layer represents the grid as a graph of buses, transmission lines, and protection zones. A fault at a node propagates according to the network's electrical topology and the protection settings. The financial layer represents correlation in financing conditions, customer concentration, and equipment supply chains, which are not topological but are also not captured by a simple copula on failure indicators.

The joint failure probability for a set of facilities \mathcal{S} is

\Pr\left(\bigcap_{i \in \mathcal{S}} F_i\right) = \mathbb{E}_{\text{fault}}\left[\prod_{i \in \mathcal{S}} \mathbf{1}\{\text{facility } i \text{ disconnects} \mid \text{fault}, \text{protection regime}\}\right] \cdot C_{\nu,\rho}^{t}\left(p_1,\ldots,p_n\right),

where the first factor is the topological propagation probability conditional on the fault and the protection regime, and the second factor is a residual copula for the financial and supply-chain correlation that is not mediated by the grid. The copula is no longer the primary model; it is the residual.

6.3 Protection settings as a policy lever

The NERC evidence indicates that protection-setting changes reduced a repeat event from approximately 1,500 MW to roughly 500 MW. This means that the dependence parameter is not a fixed property of geography; it is a function of the protection regime. The framework must therefore condition on the regime:

\rho = \rho(\text{protection settings}, \text{voltage ride-through standards}, \text{post-fault recovery requirements}).

This is a crucial correction to the v0.3 identification strategy. The v0.3 estimator of \rho^* assumed that \rho could be estimated from observed joint failures and then inverted. But if \rho is a policy lever, the observed \rho is an equilibrium outcome of past regulatory choices, not a structural parameter. The correct object of estimation is the mapping from protection regimes to \rho, and the policy question is which regime achieves \rho < \rho^*.

