7. Identification

7.1 The \rho^* threshold

The v0.3 estimator rested on approximately three events in one Virginia cluster. With three events, neither the tail dependence \nu nor the distance dependence of joint failure can be estimated with useful precision. The estimator is therefore revised in three ways.

First, the estimation sample must be expanded beyond the Ashburn cluster to include events in other interconnections, other voltage levels, and other protection regimes. The NERC disturbance record from 2024–2026 is the natural sampling frame, but the unit of observation is the fault-facility pair, not the event, because the object of interest is the probability that a facility disconnects conditional on a fault at a given electrical distance.

Second, the estimator must condition on the protection regime. The identifying variation comes from regime changes—settings updates, ride-through standard revisions, and post-fault recovery requirements—that alter the propagation probability without altering the underlying grid topology. This is a difference-in-differences design at the facility level, not a cross-sectional inversion.

Third, \rho^* is defined relative to the survivability functional, not relative to the copula parameter alone:

\rho^* = \inf\left\{\rho : \mathcal{V}_D^{(k)}(\rho) \le \mathcal{V}_C^{(k)}(\rho)\right\},

where \mathcal{V}_D^{(k)} and \mathcal{V}_C^{(k)} are the survivability of distributed and centralised architectures for workload class k. The threshold is estimated by computing the survivability functional at each candidate \rho and finding the crossing point. This is more demanding than inverting a copula, but it is the quantity the hypothesis actually concerns.

7.2 The rebound elasticity

The v0.3 instrumental-variable strategy used architecture transitions as instruments for efficiency. This is fragile: architecture transitions improve memory bandwidth, interconnect bandwidth, and numerical precision support alongside performance-per-watt, so they can shift demand through capability channels that violate the exclusion restriction. With roughly six architecture generations and concurrent demand growth, the instrument is weak and likely invalid.

Two alternative identification strategies are proposed. The first uses regional electricity-price variation as an instrument for the effective cost of compute. Electricity prices vary for reasons unrelated to AI demand—fuel prices, weather, transmission constraints—and they affect the cost of running inference and training without directly affecting the capability frontier. The exclusion restriction is that electricity prices affect compute demand only through the cost channel, not through capability. This is plausible for inference, less so for frontier training, where capability is the binding constraint.

The second uses price-per-token panels to estimate the elasticity of demand with respect to serving cost. If token prices fall exogenously—because of hardware improvements, software optimisation, or competitive entry—the demand response can be estimated from the panel. The challenge is separating the supply-side price decline from the demand-side shift; a shift-share design using regional hardware vintage as the share and national price movements as the shift is one approach.

7.3 The falsification condition

The v0.3 falsification condition used "ISA" (interconnection service agreement), which is a generator concept, and an arbitrary survival threshold of 0.7. It also used a 2025–2027 window that cannot resolve until 2028.

The corrected falsification condition is stated in terms of the commercial operation date (COD), not the ISA, and uses a survival estimator with withdrawal as a competing risk. Let T be the time from interconnection request to COD, and let W be the time to withdrawal. The object of interest is the cumulative incidence of COD:

F_{\mathrm{COD}}(t) = \Pr(T \le t, \text{no withdrawal before } t).

The hypothesis H1″ is falsified in region g and window [t_1,t_2] if the cumulative incidence of COD at 48 months, conditional on an executed interconnection agreement and controlling for construction-permit time, exceeds a benchmark calibrated to the pre-2020 cohort. The benchmark is not an arbitrary 0.7; it is the observed pre-2020 cumulative incidence, which is the appropriate counterfactual for the claim that the current pipeline is abnormally slow.

