8. Policy Instruments

The framework's strongest claim is that stranded capital is the policy-relevant quantity. The following instruments follow directly from the analytical structure and are specified at the level of reporting and regulatory design.

8.1 Disclosure of unenergised inventory and contractual exposure

Require hyperscalers and neoclouds to disclose, in quarterly and annual filings, (i) the carrying value of installed but unenergised accelerator inventory, (ii) the aggregate take-or-pay obligations associated with that inventory, and (iii) the depreciation schedule applied to AI accelerators relative to secondary-market residual values. This is a narrow disclosure that makes \Sigma observable without requiring a new accounting standard. The gap between the book clock and the resale clock is an unrecognised economic liability; disclosure is the minimum condition for pricing it.

8.2 Lead-Time Mismatch Index

Require the Department of Energy, FERC, and NERC to publish a quarterly Lead-Time Mismatch Index by region, comparing median energisation lead times to median hardware depreciation half-lives. The index makes the temporal constraint visible in the same way that reserve margins are visible. It is constructed from data that already exist—interconnection queues, equipment lead-time surveys, and secondary-market prices—and it does not require new modelling.

8.3 Demonstrated-commitment queue reform

Tie queue priority to demonstrated equipment procurement and financial commitment, not to speculative requests. The ERCOT and PJM queues are congested with projects that will never be built; a demonstrated-commitment requirement would shorten the effective queue and reduce the lead-time mismatch for serious projects. This is already partially happening—ERCOT's audit and PJM's co-location framework are steps in this direction—but the principle should be generalised: queue position should reflect the probability of completion, not the date of application.

8.4 Protection-regime standards as resilience policy

Because \rho is a function of protection settings, ride-through standards, and post-fault recovery requirements, resilience policy is not a matter of exhortation. NERC's Project 2026-02 and 2026-03 are the relevant instruments: they define the modelling and performance requirements that determine whether a facility disconnects during a fault. The policy objective is to set the regime such that the implied \rho is below the estimated \rho^* for the workload classes that matter.

8.5 Flexibility procurement institutions

The decomposition \phi = \phi_{\mathrm{contract}} \cdot \phi_{\mathrm{workload}}(k) is conceptually correct but institutionally incomplete. \phi_{\mathrm{contract}} is not a design variable; it is the output of a bargaining process between the operator, the customer, the grid operator, and the regulator. The framework should therefore specify the contractual forms that make flexibility credible: dynamic operating envelopes with penalties, capacity accreditation for curtailable load, and standardised curtailment products. Without these, flexibility remains a mathematical variable that no one has the authority to set.

