9. Multiplanetary Economic Closure

9.1 Physical closure

For delivered IT power P_{\mathrm{IT}}, the radiator area is

A_{\mathrm{rad}} = \frac{P_{\mathrm{IT}}}{\epsilon \sigma T_{\mathrm{rad}}^4},

and the storage mass is

M_{\mathrm{bat}} = \frac{P_{\mathrm{IT}} \, t_{\mathrm{night}}}{e_{\mathrm{bat}}}.

Turyshev's base case gives A_{\mathrm{rad}} = 2.50 \times 10^3 \, \mathrm{m}^2 for a 1 MW high-sunlight anchor, with photovoltaic, storage, and radiator mass at 29.4 \, \mathrm{kg/kW} and total spacecraft mass at 34\text{–}59 \, \mathrm{kg/kW}. The GAO spotlight is qualitative and does not corroborate the 1,200–6,000 m²/MW range; published benchmarks span roughly 400–2,500 m²/MW, which places Turyshev's figure at the high end of the credible range, not at its centre. The physical closure condition is

M_{\mathrm{total}} = \sum_i M_i \le M_{\mathrm{launch}}.

9.2 Economic closure

A node has positive capacity for civilisation-level coordination only if physical closure holds and

M_{\mathrm{total}} \, c_{\mathrm{launch}} + C_{\mathrm{ops}} < \mathrm{Value}(C_i) \cdot U_{\mathrm{eff}} \cdot \Pi_{\mathrm{life}},

where c_{\mathrm{launch}} is combined launch and spacecraft-build cost per kilogram, U_{\mathrm{eff}} is the effective utilisation of delivered compute, and \Pi_{\mathrm{life}} is the lifetime penalty. At m_{\mathrm{kW}} \approx 40 \, \mathrm{kg/kW} and a terrestrial benchmark of 10–40 k$/kW, the allowable combined launch-plus-build cost is roughly 250–1,000 $/kg. This is 3.4 to 13.5 times below the current public Falcon 9 dedicated LEO launch price alone, before spacecraft build.

The threshold is not a forecast. It is a necessary condition. The binding constraint is thermal rejection and launch cost, not electricity generation, and the credible early regimes are space-native preprocessing and communications-integrated edge compute, where the alternative is not terrestrial compute but downlink bandwidth.

9.3 Launch-slot scarcity

The economic closure condition treats launch cost as a price per kilogram in a competitive market with elastic supply. In reality, launch slots are scarce and allocated by priority. Even if launch cost fell to 250 $/kg, a civilisation-level orbital compute programme would compete with national-security payloads, scientific missions, and commercial constellations for a limited number of launch opportunities. The relevant constraint is not just dollars per kilogram but dollars and slot availability. The revised condition is

M_{\mathrm{total}}\left(c_{\mathrm{launch}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{launch}}\right) + C_{\mathrm{ops}} < \mathrm{Value}(C_i) \cdot U_{\mathrm{eff}} \cdot \Pi_{\mathrm{life}},

where \lambda_{\mathrm{launch}} is the scarcity rent on a launch slot. This raises the effective threshold above 250 $/kg and makes the framework more realistic about the institutional barriers to orbital compute.

