Appendix A — Existence of Bistability in the Financing-Constrained Inventory System

A.1 Setting

Let I(t) \ge 0 denote unenergised inventory and let the inventory dynamics be

\dot I = u_H - G(I), \qquad
G(I) := a_0 Q\, \Psi\big(r(I)\big) + \big[\theta_0 + \theta_1 r(I) I\big] I,

with u_H, a_0, Q, \theta_0 > 0, \theta_1 \ge 0, r: [0,\infty) \to (0,\infty) the project-weighted cost of capital, and \Psi: (0,\infty) \to (0,\infty) the financing-constrained service intensity.

A.2 Structural assumptions

(A1) G \in C^2([0,\infty)), G(I) \to \infty as I \to \infty.

(A2) There exist 0 < I_a < I_b such that

G'(I) > 0 \ \text{on } [0,I_a), \quad G'(I_a)=0, \quad G'(I) < 0 \ \text{on } (I_a,I_b), \quad G'(I_b)=0, \quad G'(I) > 0 \ \text{on } (I_b,\infty).

Assumption (A2) is the S-shape condition: G has a local maximum at I_a and a local minimum at I_b.

A.3 Proposition 1 (restated formally)

Under (A1)–(A2), for every u_H \in \big(G(I_b),\, G(I_a)\big) the system admits exactly three equilibria I_L^* < I_U^* < I_H^*. I_L^* and I_H^* are locally asymptotically stable; I_U^* is unstable. The unstable equilibrium I_U^* is the tipping threshold.

Proof. On [0,I_a), G is continuous and strictly increasing with G(0) \ge 0 < u_H < G(I_a); by the intermediate value theorem there is a unique I_L^* \in (0,I_a) with G(I_L^*) = u_H. On (I_a,I_b), G decreases strictly from G(I_a) to G(I_b), and G(I_b) < u_H < G(I_a); hence a unique I_U^* \in (I_a,I_b) with G(I_U^*)=u_H. On (I_b,\infty), G increases strictly from G(I_b) to +\infty, giving a unique I_H^* \in (I_b,\infty).

Stability: the linearisation of \dot I = u_H - G(I) at an equilibrium is -G'(I^*). Hence I_L^* and I_H^* are stable (G' > 0) and I_U^* is unstable (G' < 0). \square

A.4 Lemma A.1 (sufficient parametric condition for the S-shape)

Let \Psi(r) = e^{-\lambda r} with \lambda > 0, and r(I) = r_0 + c I^{\gamma} with r_0, c > 0 and \gamma > 1. Define the financing pressure

J(I) := a_0 Q\,\lambda c \gamma\, I^{\gamma - 1} e^{-\lambda (r_0 + c I^{\gamma})}

and the abandonment-and-baseline pressure

P(I) := \theta_0 + \theta_1 c\gamma\, I^{\gamma+1} + 2\theta_1\big(r_0 + c I^{\gamma}\big) I .

Then G'(I) = P(I) - J(I). A sufficient condition for (A2) is that the maximum of J over (0,\infty) strictly exceeds the infimum of P over the neighbourhood of its maximiser, i.e.

\max_{I>0} J(I) \;>\; \inf_{I>0} P(I) .

Proof. Direct differentiation gives G'(I) = P(I) - J(I). Because \gamma > 1, J(0^+)=0 and J(I) \to 0 as I \to \infty, while J is unimodal with a unique maximiser at

I^\star = \left(\frac{\gamma - 1}{\lambda c \gamma}\right)^{1/\gamma}.

P is strictly increasing in I for I > 0. Therefore if J(I^\star) > P(I^\star), there is an open interval around I^\star on which G' < 0; since G'(0^+) = \theta_0 > 0 and G'(I) \to +\infty as I \to \infty, G' changes sign twice, giving the two critical points I_a < I^\star < I_b required by (A2). \square

A.5 Remark on the v0.3 defect

In v0.3 the service rate a was treated as independent of I, so that

G_{0.3}(I) = a + \theta_0 I + \theta_1 r I^2, \qquad G_{0.3}'(I) = \theta_0 + 2\theta_1 r I > 0,

which is strictly increasing and admits exactly one stable equilibrium. The v0.3 claim of a threshold therefore had no support in the v0.3 equation. The threshold appears only when the service term is itself decreasing in inventory through the financing channel, which is the mechanism formalised in (A2) and Lemma A.1.

