Appendix B — Activity-Network Algebra and Copula Construction

B.1 Path algebra

Let \mathcal{A} be a finite set of activities with durations D_i > 0 and precedence relation \prec defining a directed acyclic graph \mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{A}, \prec). Let \mathcal{P} be the set of maximal directed paths from source to sink. The project completion time is

T_{\mathrm{op}} = t_0 + \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}} \sum_{i \in p} D_i .

For a fixed path set, T_{\mathrm{op}} is a max of linear functions of \mathbf{D}, hence convex and positively homogeneous in \mathbf{D}. It is therefore Lipschitz with constant at most the length of the longest path in activity count, and its distribution can be computed by inclusion–exclusion or by Monte Carlo on the copula.

B.2 Sensitivity of the completion time

The path-criticality indicator is

\chi_p(\mathbf{D}) = \mathbf{1}\Big\{ p = \arg\max_{p' \in \mathcal{P}} \sum_{i \in p'} D_i \Big\}.

With probability one, the maximiser is unique. The gradient of T_{\mathrm{op}} with respect to D_i is \chi_{p(i)}(\mathbf{D}), the indicator that the path containing i is critical. Thus

\frac{\partial T_{\mathrm{op}}}{\partial D_i} = \sum_{p \ni i} \chi_p(\mathbf{D}).

This is the formal statement that a delay in a non-critical activity has no effect on energisation until that activity becomes critical. It is the mechanism by which correlated durations propagate: a correlation between two activities on the same path transmits one-for-one into T_{\mathrm{op}}, while a correlation between activities on different paths transmits only through the criticality indicator.

B.3 Vine copula specification

Let d = |\mathcal{A}|. A regular vine on d elements is a sequence of trees \mathcal{T}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{T}_{d-1} with edge sets E_1, \ldots, E_{d-1} satisfying the proximity condition. The joint density is

f(\mathbf{D}) = \prod_{i=1}^{d} f_i(D_i) \prod_{k=1}^{d-1} \prod_{e \in E_k} c_{e;\, e_a, e_b}\!\Big(F_{e_a \mid e_b}(D_{e_a} \mid D_{e_b}),\, F_{e_b \mid e_a}(D_{e_b} \mid D_{e_a})\Big),

where c_{e;\,e_a,e_b} is the pair-copula on edge e with conditioned variables e_a, e_b and conditioning set the remaining elements of e.

Three pair-copula families are admissible: the Gaussian (no tail dependence), the Student-t (symmetric tail dependence), and the Gumbel (upper-tail dependence). The choice is constrained by the mechanism: shared equipment supply chains generate upper-tail dependence (joint long delays), whereas shared financing generates symmetric dependence (both short and long delays propagate). The specification therefore assigns a Gumbel pair-copula to the supply-chain edges and a Student-t pair-copula to the financing edges, with the degrees of freedom estimated from the empirical tail.

B.4 Energisation lag

The lag is \Delta_L = T_{\mathrm{op}} - t_{\mathrm{hardware\ available}}, where t_{\mathrm{hardware\ available}} is the completion time of the sub-network terminating at hardware installation. Because both terms are path maxima on the same network, \Delta_L is itself a max of path differences and inherits the copula dependence. Its distribution is computed by the same Monte Carlo as T_{\mathrm{op}}.

B.5 Precedence constraints

The precedence relation encodes re-sequencing. Construction B may proceed in parallel with interconnection study G_s, but energisation requires both B and G_u to be complete, and commissioning C requires hardware H and B. The relevant paths are therefore of the form G_s \to G_u \to C and H \to B \to C. A delay in G_u does not delay the hardware path but delays energisation through the commissioning gate; this is the formal content of the statement that a transformer delay blocks commissioning even when the shell is complete.

