Appendix C — Stranded Capital: Derivation and Non-Double-Counting

C.1 Flow definition

Let unit i of hardware become available at t_i^H and be energised at t_i^E, with t_i^E \ge t_i^H. Let \pi(t) be the contribution margin per unit of energised capacity per unit time (revenue net of variable operating cost including energy), and c_{\mathrm{idle}} the carrying cost of idle hardware (warehousing, insurance, financing). Define the available-but-unenergised count

A(t) := \sum_i \mathbf{1}\{ t_i^H \le t < t_i^E \}.

The flow component of stranded capital over horizon T is

\Sigma^{\mathrm{flow}}(T) = \int_0^T e^{-rt} \big[\pi(t) - c_{\mathrm{idle}}\big]\, A(t)\, dt .

The contractual component is

\Sigma^{\mathrm{contract}}(T) = \int_0^T e^{-rt}\, \kappa_c\, I_c(t)\, v_c\, dt ,

where I_c(t) is the fraction of capacity under take-or-pay obligations, v_c the contractual value per capacity unit, and \kappa_c the contractual rate. The total is

\Sigma(T) = \Sigma^{\mathrm{flow}}(T) + \Sigma^{\mathrm{contract}}(T).

C.2 Lemma C.1 (non-double-counting)

Let v_h be the purchase value of a unit and let R_v(a) be its market residual value at age a. Then the accounting depreciation charge \kappa_h I(t) v_h is not a component of \Sigma(T); it is a stock revaluation that occurs whether or not the unit is energised.

Proof. Suppose unit i is energised at t_i^E = t_i^H (no delay). Then A(t) = 0 on all t, so \Sigma^{\mathrm{flow}}(T) = 0, while the accounting depreciation over any interval [t_i^H, t_i^H + \Delta] is strictly positive if R_v(\Delta) < v_h. Since the accounting charge is positive on a path with zero stranded flow, the charge cannot be a component of the flow. Conversely, the flow is positive on a path with zero accounting charge if \pi(t) > c_{\mathrm{idle}} and R_v is flat over the delay. The two quantities are therefore distinct and neither contains the other. \square

C.3 Stock definition (separate quantity)

The v0.3 quantity that the accounting literature actually measures is an unrecognised impairment:

\mathrm{Imp}(T) = \sum_i \Big[ v_{h,i} - R_{v(i)}\big(\min(t_i^E,T) - t_i^H\big) \Big]^{+} \mathbf{1}\{ t_i^H \le T \},

the gap between purchase value and current market residual value at the valuation date. This is the correct object for the claim that the resale clock runs faster than the book clock. It is a stock, it is reported at a date, and it is not integrated over time. The main text's policy instrument 1 (disclosure of unenergised inventory and the gap between book and resale schedules) is a disclosure of \mathrm{Imp}, not of \Sigma.

C.4 Vintage aggregation

Partition inventory into vintages v \in \mathcal{V}, with n_v(t) units of vintage v available and unenergised. Then

\Sigma^{\mathrm{flow}}(T) = \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}} \int_0^T e^{-rt} \big[\pi_v(t) - c_{\mathrm{idle},v}\big]\, n_v(t)\, dt .

The vintage-specific contribution margin \pi_v differs across vintages because older hardware has lower throughput per unit power and therefore lower revenue per unit time. The residual-value curve R_v(a) enters \mathrm{Imp}, not \Sigma. This is the formal counterpart of the main text's rejection of a single depreciation rate: the correct aggregation is over vintages with vintage-specific flows, not over a scalar \kappa_h.

C.5 Stress parameterisation of the contractual rate

Because \kappa_c is not publicly observable at the required granularity, define the ratio

\zeta := \frac{\Sigma^{\mathrm{contract}}(T)}{\Sigma^{\mathrm{flow}}(T)}

and report \Sigma(T; \zeta) = \Sigma^{\mathrm{flow}}(T)(1+\zeta) over a grid of \zeta. The financial viability condition \Sigma(T) \le \Sigma_{\max} then defines, for each \zeta, a maximum admissible delay distribution. This converts an unobservable parameter into a sensitivity axis and is the formal content of the main text's decision to treat \kappa_c as a stress parameter.

