Appendix D — Survivability: Well-Posedness, Certified Bounds, and Discretisation Error

D.1 Fast subsystem

Let the fast state be x = (E, B, P) \in \mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^3, with E energy reserve, B storage state of charge, P delivered power. The controlled dynamics are

x_{k+1} = F(x_k, u_k, w_k), \qquad u_k \in \mathcal{U}(x_k), \quad w_k \in \mathcal{W},

with F continuous in (x,u) and Lipschitz in x with constant L_F, \mathcal{U}(x) compact-valued and upper-hemicontinuous, and \mathcal{W} compact. The safe set is

\mathcal{S} = \{ x : C_{\mathrm{eff}}(x, x_s) \ge C_{\mathrm{crit}}^{(1)} \},

assumed closed. The survivability functional is

\mathcal{V}(x_0, T) = \sup_{\{u_k\}} \Pr\big( x_k \in \mathcal{S} \ \ \forall k = 0, \ldots, T \ \big| \ x_0 \big).

D.2 Well-posedness

Proposition D.1. Under the stated assumptions, the backward recursion

V_T(x) = \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{S}}(x), \qquad
V_k(x) = \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{S}}(x) \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}(x)} \mathbb{E}\big[ V_{k+1}(x_{k+1}) \,\big|\, x, u \big]

is well defined, each V_k is Borel measurable and upper semi-analytic, and V_0(x_0) = \mathcal{V}(x_0,T).

Proof sketch. The transition kernel Q(dx' \mid x, u) is a continuous function of (x,u) in the weak topology because F is continuous and \mathcal{W} is compact. Upper semi-analyticity is preserved under countable suprema and integrals against kernels (Bertsekas–Shreve, Stochastic Optimal Control, Prop. 7.2 and Cor. 7.5). Compactness of \mathcal{U}(x) and upper hemicontinuity give the existence of a measurable selector attaining the supremum. The equality V_0 = \mathcal{V} follows by induction on k using the tower property. \square

D.3 The v0.3 recursion defect

The v0.3 recursion V_t = \min\{\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{S}^c}, \mathbb{E}[V_{t+1}]\} satisfies V_t \equiv 0 on \mathcal{S} because \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{S}^c} = 0 there. It is therefore identically zero and cannot represent a survival probability. It also contains no control. The corrected recursion of Proposition D.1 replaces the minimum by a product with the safe indicator and adds a supremum over admissible controls.

D.4 Certified lower bound by viability erosion

Let \mathcal{K}_0 = \mathcal{S} and

\mathcal{K}_{n+1} = \big\{ x \in \mathcal{K}_n : \exists u \in \mathcal{U}(x), \ F(x,u,\mathcal{W}) \subseteq \mathcal{K}_n \big\},

where F(x,u,\mathcal{W}) := \{ F(x,u,w) : w \in \mathcal{W} \}. The maximal controlled invariant set is \mathcal{K}_\infty = \bigcap_{n \ge 0} \mathcal{K}_n. For a set \mathcal{K} and a set \mathcal{W}, the Minkowski erosion is \mathcal{K} \ominus \mathcal{W} = \{ x : x + \mathcal{W} \subseteq \mathcal{K} \}, and the T-fold erosion is \mathcal{K} \ominus^{T} \mathcal{W} = \{x : \forall w_1,\ldots,w_T \in \mathcal{W}, \ x + \sum_{k=1}^{T} w_k \in \mathcal{K}\}.

Proposition D.2 (certified lower bound). Suppose F is additively separable in the disturbance, F(x,u,w) = f(x,u) + w with w \in \mathcal{W}. Then for every x_0 \in \mathcal{K}_\infty \ominus^{T} \mathcal{W}, \mathcal{V}(x_0,T) = 1. More generally, if x_0 \in \mathcal{K}_\infty \ominus^{T} \mathcal{W} fails only for a subset of disturbance sequences of probability at most \alpha, then \mathcal{V}(x_0,T) \ge 1 - \alpha.

Proof. Under additive separability, the T-step reachable set from x_0 under a fixed policy is x_T \in \Phi_T(x_0) + \sum_{k} w_k. If x_0 \in \mathcal{K}_\infty \ominus^T \mathcal{W}, then for every disturbance sequence the trajectory remains in \mathcal{K}_\infty \subseteq \mathcal{S}, so survival is certain. The probabilistic refinement follows by applying the same argument to the complement of the measure-\alpha exceptional set. \square

D.5 Discretisation error

Let \mathcal{G}_h be a grid on \mathcal{X} with cell diameter h. Define the pessimistic and optimistic operators

(\underline{T}_h V)(x) = \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{S}}(x) \sup_{u} \min_{x' \in \mathcal{N}_h(x)} \mathbb{E}[V(x') \mid x,u],

(\overline{T}_h V)(x) = \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{S}}(x) \sup_{u} \max_{x' \in \mathcal{N}_h(x)} \mathbb{E}[V(x') \mid x,u],

where \mathcal{N}_h(x) is the set of neighbouring cells.

Proposition D.3 (error bound). Assume V_k is Lipschitz on \mathcal{X} with constant L_V uniformly in k. Then for all k,

\underline{V}_k(x) \le V_k(x) \le \overline{V}_k(x) \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{G}_h,
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\sup_{x} \big| \overline{V}_k(x) - \underline{V}_k(x) \big| \le 2 L_V h .

Proof. Monotonicity of the Bellman operator in the value function, together with the fact that the pessimistic operator uses the minimum over the cell neighbourhood and the optimistic the maximum, gives the two-sided inequality by induction from the terminal condition, where the inequality is tight. The gap bound follows from the Lipschitz property: the diameter of \mathcal{N}_h(x) is at most 2h, so the difference between the maximum and minimum of V over the neighbourhood is at most 2L_V h. \square

The deliverable of the empirical programme is therefore the pair (\underline{V}_0, \overline{V}_0) at representative initial states, together with the Monte Carlo estimate \hat{V}_0^{MC} with N = 10^4 paths and a binomial confidence interval. The quantity of interest is the triple (\underline{V}, \overline{V}, \hat{V}^{MC}), reported as a function of the grid resolution. A bound-to-Monte-Carlo gap that shrinks with h at the rate predicted by Proposition D.3 is a certificate that the discretisation is consistent; a gap that does not shrink indicates that the Lipschitz assumption fails and the grid must be refined adaptively.

