Appendix E — Topological Common-Mode Failure and the \rho^* Functional

E.1 Grid graph and fault propagation

Let the electrical network be a graph \mathcal{G}_E = (\mathcal{N}, \mathcal{E}) with buses \mathcal{N} and branches \mathcal{E}, together with a protection map \pi: \mathcal{N} \cup \mathcal{E} \to \Pi assigning a protection setting to each element, where \Pi is a finite set of regimes. A fault at bus f induces a voltage and frequency trajectory; the set of buses that disconnect is determined by the protection responses, the voltage ride-through characteristics of the loads, and the post-fault recovery requirements.

Define the disconnection indicator for facility i under fault f and regime \pi:

D_i(f, \pi) = \mathbf{1}\big\{ \text{facility } i \text{ trips under fault } f \text{ with protection regime } \pi \big\}.

E.2 Cut-set representation

For a set \mathcal{S} of facilities, the event of simultaneous disconnection is contained in the event that the fault is in the common cut set of \mathcal{S}: the set of network elements whose failure disconnects all facilities in \mathcal{S} simultaneously. Let \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}) be the family of minimal cut sets. Then

\Pr\Big( \bigcap_{i \in \mathcal{S}} D_i \Big) \approx \sum_{C \in \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S})} \Pr\big( \text{fault in } C \big) \cdot \Pr\big( \text{propagation} \mid C, \pi \big).

This representation makes the mechanism explicit: the joint failure probability is driven by shared cut sets, not by residual correlation between facility indicators. Facilities on the same radial corridor share a cut set; facilities on separate corridors with distinct protection zones do not.

E.3 Two-layer factorisation

The main text's factorisation is

\Pr\Big( \bigcap_{i \in \mathcal{S}} F_i \Big) = \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{\text{fault}}\Big[ \prod_{i \in \mathcal{S}} D_i(f,\pi) \Big]}_{\text{topological layer}} \cdot \underbrace{C^{t}_{\nu,\rho}\big(p_1,\ldots,p_n\big)}_{\text{residual financial layer}} .

The first factor is computed from the graph and the protection map; it is not a copula object. The second factor captures correlation that is not mediated by the grid: shared financing conditions, shared customer concentration, shared equipment supply chains. The copula is the residual, not the primary model.

E.4 The \rho^* functional

Define the survivability functional for architecture A \in \{C, D\} (centralised, distributed), workload class k, and dependence parameter \rho:

\mathcal{V}_A^{(k)}(\rho) = V_0^{(A,k)}\big(x_0;\, \rho\big),

computed by the recursion of Appendix D with the joint failure distribution given by Appendix E.3. Define

\rho^*_k = \inf\big\{ \rho \in [0,1] : \mathcal{V}_D^{(k)}(\rho) \le \mathcal{V}_C^{(k)}(\rho) \big\}.

Proposition E.1. Suppose \mathcal{V}_D^{(k)} is non-increasing in \rho and \mathcal{V}_C^{(k)} is non-decreasing in \rho. Then \rho^*_k exists and the comparison \mathcal{R}_D^{(k)} > \mathcal{R}_C^{(k)} holds iff \rho < \rho^*_k.

The monotonicity is the substantive assumption. It is plausible because increasing dependence raises the joint failure probability and therefore harms the architecture with the larger number of exposed components (the distributed one) more than the architecture with fewer components (the centralised one), provided the centralised architecture's grid-side flexibility is not itself dependent on the same corridor. The assumption should be tested numerically in the second paper.

E.5 Conditioning on the protection regime

Because the NERC record shows that protection-setting changes reduced a repeat event from approximately 1.5 GW to roughly 0.5 GW, the dependence parameter is a function of the regime:

\rho = \rho(\pi).

The identifying variation for \rho is therefore variation in \pi across time and jurisdiction, not cross-sectional variation in observed joint failures. The estimation is a difference-in-differences at the facility level:

\Delta_{i,t} = \alpha_i + \gamma_t + \beta\, \mathbf{1}\{\pi_t = \pi'\} + \varepsilon_{i,t},

where \Delta_{i,t} is the disconnection indicator for facility i in event t, \alpha_i and \gamma_t are facility and event fixed effects, and \pi' is the post-change regime. The coefficient \beta identifies the change in propagation probability attributable to the regime change, conditional on the event.

