Appendix F — Identification Assumptions

F.1 The \rho^* estimator

Assumption F.1 (sampling frame). The fault-facility pairs are drawn from the NERC disturbance record 2024–2026 and are representative of the fault types and voltage levels that dominate the joint-failure risk.

Assumption F.2 (regime exogeneity). The timing of protection-regime changes is uncorrelated with the unobserved determinants of facility disconnection, conditional on facility and event fixed effects.

Assumption F.3 (no anticipation). Facilities do not adjust ride-through settings in anticipation of a regime change.

Under F.1–F.3, the difference-in-differences estimator of Appendix E.5 is consistent for the regime effect on propagation probability. The estimated propagation probabilities are then inserted into the cut-set representation and the survivability functional is computed at each candidate \rho.

Remark F.1. With approximately three events in the Ashburn cluster, neither the tail dependence \nu nor the distance dependence of joint failure can be estimated from that cluster alone. The estimator requires the expanded sampling frame, and the reported \rho^* must carry an interval that reflects the sampling uncertainty in the propagation probabilities. A point estimate of \rho^* without an interval would overstate the precision of the underlying evidence.

F.2 The rebound elasticity

Assumption F.4 (instrument relevance). Regional electricity prices are correlated with the effective cost of compute, conditional on region and time fixed effects.

Assumption F.5 (exclusion). Regional electricity prices affect compute demand only through the cost channel, not through the capability frontier.

Assumption F.6 (monotonicity). The price response of compute demand is monotone in the instrument; regions facing higher prices do not increase compute demand for reasons unrelated to cost.

Under F.4–F.6, the two-stage least-squares estimator of \varepsilon in

\log W_{r,t} = \alpha_r + \gamma_t + \varepsilon \log I_{C,r,t} + \delta X_{r,t} + u_{r,t},

with \log I_{C,r,t} instrumented by regional electricity prices, is consistent. The exclusion restriction F.5 is more plausible for inference than for frontier training; the estimates should therefore be reported separately by workload class, and the training-class estimate should be flagged as less credible.

Alternative identification. A shift-share design uses regional hardware vintage shares interacted with national price movements:

Z_{r,t} = \sum_{v} s_{r,v}\, \Delta \log p^{\mathrm{nat}}_{v,t},

where s_{r,v} is the share of vintage v in region r and p^{\mathrm{nat}}_{v,t} is the national price of vintage v at time t. The identifying assumption is that regional vintage composition affects compute demand only through its effect on the effective price of compute, not through capability. This is testable by checking whether vintage shares predict demand conditional on the instrumented price.

F.3 The falsification condition

Let T be the time from interconnection request to commercial operation date and W the time to withdrawal. The cumulative incidence of commercial operation is

F_{\mathrm{COD}}(t) = \Pr\big( T \le t,\ W > t \big),

estimated by the Aalen–Johansen estimator for competing risks:

\hat{F}_{\mathrm{COD}}(t) = \sum_{t_j \le t} \hat{S}(t_j^-)\, \frac{d_j^{\mathrm{COD}}}{n_j},

where d_j^{\mathrm{COD}} is the number of commercial-operation events at t_j, n_j the number at risk, and \hat{S} the Kaplan–Meier estimator of the overall event-free survival.

Proposition F.1 (falsification). H1″ is falsified in region g and window [t_1,t_2] if

\hat{F}_{\mathrm{COD}}^{(g)}(48) \;>\; F_{\mathrm{COD}}^{\mathrm{bench}},

where F_{\mathrm{COD}}^{\mathrm{bench}} is the cumulative incidence at 48 months for the pre-2020 cohort in the same region, controlling for construction-permit time by stratification.

The benchmark is empirical, not an arbitrary threshold. The v0.3 threshold of 0.7 is discarded because it has no justification.

