Appendix G — Reference Workloads and Energy-to-Solution Units

G.1 Definition

One unit of computational capacity is the energy-to-solution of a standardised reference workload, reported separately for training and inference.

Training reference W^{\mathrm{tr}}. A single optimizer step of a 70-billion-parameter dense transformer at BF16, sequence length 4096, global batch 1024, fixed parallelism layout (t_p, p_p, d_p), and fixed optimizer state precision.

Inference reference W^{\mathrm{inf}}. A 1,000-token generation at P50 latency for a 70-billion-parameter model at INT8, fixed batch size, and fixed KV-cache precision.

G.2 Energy-to-solution

For a hardware configuration h and workload W,

E(W, h) = \int_0^{T(W,h)} P_h(t)\, dt,

where T(W,h) is the wall-clock solution time and P_h(t) the instantaneous power draw including accelerator, host, and cooling overhead. The capacity measure is

C(W,h) = \frac{1}{E(W,h)}.

G.3 Invariance requirements

The reference workloads are conventions, not universal metrics. They are admissible only if three invariance conditions hold:

1. Version stability. The specification may change only with a versioned revision, so that time series remain comparable across hardware generations.
2. Comparability. The reported capacity is comparable across architectures only if the reference workload is feasible on both; infeasible comparisons are reported as infeasible, not as zero.
3. Overhead inclusion. Cooling and host overhead are included in P_h(t). Excluding them would make the measure sensitive to PUE assumptions and would conflate computational efficiency with facility efficiency.

