Appendix H — Multiplanetary Closure Algebra

H.1 Thermal closure

For delivered IT power P_{\mathrm{IT}}, the radiator area required to reject waste heat at temperature T_{\mathrm{rad}} with emissivity \epsilon is

A_{\mathrm{rad}} = \frac{P_{\mathrm{IT}}}{\epsilon\, \sigma\, T_{\mathrm{rad}}^4},

where \sigma is the Stefan–Boltzmann constant. The radiator mass is

M_{\mathrm{rad}} = \rho_{\mathrm{rad}}\, A_{\mathrm{rad}},

with \rho_{\mathrm{rad}} the areal mass density of the radiator assembly.

H.2 Storage closure

For a night duration t_{\mathrm{night}} and storage specific energy e_{\mathrm{bat}},

M_{\mathrm{bat}} = \frac{P_{\mathrm{IT}}\, t_{\mathrm{night}}}{e_{\mathrm{bat}}} .

H.3 Mass closure

The total spacecraft mass is

M_{\mathrm{total}} = M_{\mathrm{rad}} + M_{\mathrm{bat}} + M_{\mathrm{PV}} + M_{\mathrm{struct}} + M_{\mathrm{compute}},

and physical closure requires

M_{\mathrm{total}} \le M_{\mathrm{launch}}.

At Turyshev's central values, the photovoltaic, storage, and radiator mass together are approximately 29.4 \, \mathrm{kg/kW}, and total spacecraft mass is 34–59 \, \mathrm{kg/kW}. The GAO spotlight is qualitative and does not corroborate the 1,200–6,000 m²/MW range; published benchmarks span roughly 400–2,500 m²/MW, placing Turyshev's 2.50 \times 10^3 \, \mathrm{m}^2/\mathrm{MW} at the high end of the credible range, not at its centre. The physical closure condition should therefore be evaluated across the full benchmark range, not at a single anchor.

H.4 Economic closure

Let V_{\mathrm{kW}} be the terrestrial value of one kilowatt of delivered compute over the asset life, U_{\mathrm{eff}} the effective utilisation of delivered compute, \Pi_{\mathrm{life}} the lifetime penalty (the ratio of orbital to terrestrial service life, reflecting radiation, thermal cycling, and maintenance inaccessibility), m = M_{\mathrm{total}}/P_{\mathrm{IT}} the specific mass in kg/kW, and c_{\mathrm{launch}} the combined launch and spacecraft-build cost per kilogram. Economic closure requires

m\, c_{\mathrm{launch}} + C_{\mathrm{ops}}/P_{\mathrm{IT}} \;<\; V_{\mathrm{kW}}\, U_{\mathrm{eff}}\, \Pi_{\mathrm{life}} .

Neglecting C_{\mathrm{ops}} and setting U_{\mathrm{eff}} = \Pi_{\mathrm{life}} = 1 gives the most permissive bound:

c_{\mathrm{launch}} \;<\; \frac{V_{\mathrm{kW}}}{m}.

With V_{\mathrm{kW}} \in [10,40]\, \mathrm{k\$/kW} and m \approx 40\, \mathrm{kg/kW}, the allowable combined cost is 250–1{,}000\, \mathrm{\$/kg}. With U_{\mathrm{eff}}\Pi_{\mathrm{life}} = 0.5, the bound tightens to 125–500\, \mathrm{\$/kg}. The main text's 250\, \mathrm{\$/kg} threshold is the lower edge of the most permissive bound and should be read as the point below which general-purpose terrestrial-user compute becomes economically conceivable, not as the point at which it becomes attractive.

H.5 Launch-slot scarcity

Let \lambda_{\mathrm{launch}} be the shadow price of a launch slot. The closure condition becomes

M_{\mathrm{total}}\big(c_{\mathrm{launch}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{launch}}\big) + C_{\mathrm{ops}} \;<\; V_{\mathrm{kW}}\,P_{\mathrm{IT}}\,U_{\mathrm{eff}}\,\Pi_{\mathrm{life}} .

Dividing by P_{\mathrm{IT}} gives the per-kilowatt form

m\big(c_{\mathrm{launch}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{launch}}\big) + C_{\mathrm{ops}}/P_{\mathrm{IT}} \;<\; V_{\mathrm{kW}}\,U_{\mathrm{eff}}\,\Pi_{\mathrm{life}} .

The shadow price \lambda_{\mathrm{launch}} is not observable from public launch prices; it is bounded below by the difference between the market-clearing price of a slot and the marginal cost of launch. The effective threshold is therefore above the 250\, \mathrm{\$/kg} bound by the amount m\lambda_{\mathrm{launch}}, and the main text's conclusion that general-purpose orbital compute fails closure is strengthened, not weakened, by the inclusion of slot scarcity.

