Appendix I — Algorithmic Specification of the Decisive Monte Carlo

The specification is mathematical, not computational. It defines the random objects and the functionals to be estimated; it does not specify an implementation.

I.1 Inputs

A fitted vine copula C_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} on activity durations (Appendix B.3); marginal distributions F_i (Appendix B.3); a contribution-margin function \pi_v(t) by vintage (Appendix C.4); a carrying cost c_{\mathrm{idle},v}; a discount rate r; a contractual stress ratio \zeta (Appendix C.5); and a horizon T.

I.2 Sampling

For n = 1, \ldots, N:

1. Draw \mathbf{D}^{(n)} \sim C_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(F_1,\ldots,F_d).
2. Compute T_{\mathrm{op}}^{(n)} = t_0 + \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}} \sum_{i \in p} D_i^{(n)}.
3. Compute t_{i}^{H,(n)} and t_i^{E,(n)} for each unit i from the path structure.
4. Compute A^{(n)}(t) = \sum_i \mathbf{1}\{ t_i^{H,(n)} \le t < t_i^{E,(n)} \} on a time grid.
5. Compute \Sigma^{(n)}(T) = \int_0^T e^{-rt}[\pi(t) - c_{\mathrm{idle}}] A^{(n)}(t)\, dt \cdot (1+\zeta).

I.3 Estimands

Report the distribution of \Sigma(T) over the N draws: the mean, the median, the 90th and 99th percentiles, and the probability \Pr(\Sigma(T) > \Sigma_{\max}). Report the same quantities as a function of \zeta on a grid, producing a family of survival curves.

I.4 Convergence

The estimator of \Pr(\Sigma(T) > \Sigma_{\max}) is a binomial proportion; the half-width of a 1-\alpha confidence interval is z_{1-\alpha/2}\sqrt{\hat p(1-\hat p)/N}. For N = 10^4 and \hat p = 0.1, the half-width is approximately 0.006. Report N and the half-width with every estimate.

I.5 Scope

This simulation is the decisive one for the financial result. The survivability dynamic program of Appendix D and the topological common-mode model of Appendix E belong to a second paper, where they can be developed at the resolution their assumptions require. Combining them into a single "smallest" simulation, as v0.3 attempted, produces an exercise that is neither small nor decisive.

