Appendix J — Verification Protocol and Error Taxonomy

J.1 Status categories

Every anchor carries one of three statuses.

Primary. Verified against the originating document: a regulatory filing, a statistical release, a peer-reviewed or preprint record, a formal standard, or a public agency report. The anchor may be cited as a datum.

Secondary. Reported by a credible intermediary (trade press, consultancy, analyst note) without independent access to the originating document. The anchor may be used only with a stated interval and must not be used as a calibration target.

Unverified. Asserted in the literature but not located in the originating text during verification. The anchor must not be used in any quantitative claim; it may be mentioned only as a claim under review.

J.2 Error taxonomy

Four error classes are distinguished.

Type I (mathematical). A derivation or recursion that does not hold as stated. Examples in v0.3: the non-monotone abandonment equation presented as bistable; the survivability recursion that returns zero everywhere; the stranded-capital integral that double counts depreciation; the conditional maximum T_{\mathrm{op}} = \max\{\cdot \mid L_G\} that is undefined without a copula specification.

Type II (empirical). A numerical claim that does not survive verification against the originating document. Examples in v0.3: the 24–27% depreciation rate presented as a single estimate; the GAO 1,200–6,000 m²/MW range; the Nscale loss presented as stranded capital; the ERCOT data-centre share.

Type III (identification). An estimation strategy whose assumptions are not credible for the data at hand. Examples in v0.3: the \rho^* estimator resting on three events in one cluster; the architecture-transition instrument for the rebound elasticity.

Type IV (institutional). A policy or design variable treated as exogenous when it is the output of a bargaining or regulatory process. Example in v0.3: \phi_{\mathrm{contract}} treated as a design choice.

J.3 Remediation mapping

Each error class maps to a specific remedial action. Type I errors are corrected by re-derivation, as in Appendices A, C, and D. Type II errors are corrected by demotion in the claims ledger, as in §1.2. Type III errors are corrected by re-specification of the estimator, as in Appendices E and F. Type IV errors are corrected by adding the institutional layer to the model, as in §8.5. A claim that cannot be remediated by any of these four routes must be withdrawn.

